Beryllium: thread names reach the kernel on FreeBSD

Purpose: Chromium names its threads (CrRendererMain, Chrome_ChildIOThread,
  Compositor, ThreadPoolForegroundWorker, PerfettoTrace...). On Linux
  PlatformThread::SetName() also sets the kernel's name for the thread.
  The FreeBSD port's platform_thread_bsd.cc is a set of stubs whose
  SetName() calls SetNameCommon() only, which records the name inside the
  process. So the kernel knew every thread of every Beryllium process by
  the process's name: procstat -t, top -H, a debugger's thread list and a
  core file all said "beryllium" for each. Build 0.20.3.16 logged the
  kernel's names for the threads present at the renderer lockdown to find
  out which the second one was, and got thread_names=beryllium,beryllium
  (10 November 2026).
  This patch also calls pthread_set_name_np() on FreeBSD. A thread's name
  is separate from the process's name there (on Linux naming the main
  thread renames the process, which is why Chromium skips it there), so
  the main thread is named as well and ps, pkill and killall are
  unaffected. The kernel keeps the first 19 bytes of a name
  (MAXCOMLEN), so ThreadPoolForegroundWorker shows as ThreadPoolForegroun.
  The system call behind it, thr_set_name, is allowed in capability mode.
  A thread started by a library, or by Chromium without a name, still
  shows the name of the thread that started it, as on any system.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the FreeBSD port's
  patch-base_threading_platform__thread__bsd.cc (stage 2), which creates
  the file.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Compiled
  and released in 0.20.3.17 (10 November 2026): the lockdown line of four
  renderers read thread_names=CrRendererMain. pthread_set_name_np() is
  declared in <pthread_np.h>, which the port already includes in
  platform_thread_posix.cc for pthread_getthreadid_np().
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log
  prints the kernel's names for the threads at each lockdown
  (thread_names= on the lockdown line) and says when they are only the
  process's name; with this patch a renderer's reads CrRendererMain.
security-review: not required (a name for diagnostics; no input from
  outside the process)

--- a/base/threading/platform_thread_bsd.cc
+++ b/base/threading/platform_thread_bsd.cc
@@ -5,6 +5,12 @@
 
 #include "base/notimplemented.h"
 #include "base/threading/platform_thread.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
+
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+#include <pthread.h>
+#include <pthread_np.h>
+#endif
 
 namespace base {
 namespace internal {
@@ -35,6 +41,15 @@
 // static
 void PlatformThreadBase::SetName(const std::string& name) {
   SetNameCommon(name);
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+  // Beryllium: tell the kernel as well. SetNameCommon() records the name
+  // inside the process only, so procstat -t, top -H, a debugger and the
+  // renderer lockdown's own thread list knew every thread by the process's
+  // name. A thread's name is separate from the process's name on FreeBSD
+  // (unlike Linux, where naming the main thread renames the process), so
+  // the main thread is named too. The kernel keeps the first 19 bytes.
+  pthread_set_name_np(pthread_self(), name.c_str());
+#endif
 }
 
 }  // namespace base
